[{:seon.task/name "program-cost-follows-what-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "A merge from Datahike's index diff, O(changes x log V), exists only outside this repository: commit `0823517` \"seon.merge/three-way-db\" in the tag `history/l3/next/main` (its library-level numbers in the outer repository's claude_scratch/archive/option3-L3-libs.md, Item 1), which needs the datahike fork's branch ``jolt/merge-diff`` (`021e876`, `8311254`: `datahike.db/datoms-diff` on Jolt), not yet in this repository's datahike module. Fetch both before those directories are archived. What exists: `seon.merge/program` reads every live form and definition of a db (O(program), not O(change)), and the three-way merge's callers build it on base, ours and theirs per merge, per :branch-diff and in `seon.reconcile/judge-tx`. Reproduction: with Seon's own source on main (54 files, 1,168 forms, 2,278 program keys) `seon.merge/program` took 256 ms a call in the test runtime (443 ms on 1,641 forms in a release root, `seon.program/live-forms` 368 ms of it), so every run pays about 1 s of whole-program reads whatever it changed; `seon.e2e/loop-turn!` took 13.4 s from release to quiet (implement 4.8 s, merge 6.9 s), against a target under 1 s. Correct: a branch's changes since its merge base come from Datahike's history between base and head (the datoms its transactions touched), main's the same way, and only those keys are read on base, ours and theirs. Replaces the whole-program reads in three-way's callers (`seon.writer` branch-merge, `seon.reconcile/judge-tx`, the dashboard's diff); `program` stays for tests and mg/check. Done by worktree session global-ids (2026-10-23): seon.merge/delta-three-way reads the keys the datoms that differ bear (datoms-diff over shared trees, touched-keys, program-of); 6.7 s -> about 7 ms on a 115k-datom main (claude_scratch/global-ids/MEASURED.md §5), flat from 100 to 10,000 forms; every seon.merge-test case checks it answers what three-way does. Callers moved: the writer's branch-merge and :branch-diff, seon.agent/merge-main!, seon.turn's contract gaps, seon.merge/unmerged (now [db]); merges then carry the branch's own datoms (seon.merge/merge-tx)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-cost-test/a-three-way-costs-the-changes-not-the-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "conflict-value-states-its-input"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged): `seon.merge/conflict-value` decodes a pulled merge entry; its contract takes `:seon.merge/entry-members` (the :seon.merge/* members, generating as a pulled map) instead of `:any`, under which mg/check generated nil or 0 and the answer could not be a conflict map. Its callers pass a Datahike entity (`seon.merge` entries) or a pulled map (`seon.problems`, the run's conflicts; `seon.task`). Proof: mg/check green on this function, the callers' tests green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-conflict-value-reads-a-pulled-entry}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-uncontracted"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged): one owner of which functions a branch adds without a contract: `seon.merge/uncontracted` over a three-way's `:ours` (the branch's changes since its merge base), empty while contracts are off (`seon.contract/on?`). The writer's merge gate (`seon.writer` merge-refused) calls it, root's judge reads the same answer, and the agent's done check (`seon.turn`) asks it of its branch's changes. Before, the gate, the judge (`pm/program` of the branch against main's: every differing fn, contracts off too) and `seon.stage/uncontracted` (the live runtime's vars) computed it three ways and disagreed: a branch behind a main that added a contract to f was refused by the judge and admitted by the writer. Deleted: the judge's second whole-program builds and `seon.stage/uncontracted`. `seon.contract/uncontracted` (a namespace's vars without a contract) remains for the runtime question."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/uncontracted-is-what-the-branch-changed seon.reconcile-test/the-judge-and-the-merge-gate-agree-on-contract-gaps}}
 {:seon.task/name "main-wins-a-conflict"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: when an agent merges main (`seon.agent/merge-main!`) and both sides changed a key, `seon.merge/three-way` names the conflict, the branch keeps its own content, and the conflict stays an unmerged entry (`seon.merge/unmerged-tx`) until the branch redefines the key or resolves it (`seon.merge/resolve-tx`); docs/parity.md: ours is kept. v1 (MERGE-STEP 5a): main's content wins on the branch and the branch's own is kept on the entry (:seon.merge/kept), so the agent runs main's definition and re-applies its change knowingly. Correct: merge-main! applies theirs for a conflict too, the entry holds :seon.merge/kept, and `seon.merge/unmerged` keeps it open until the branch resolves it. The attribute's name waits on the owner's D6 ruling, shared with seon.merge/a-merge-task-carries-what-it-names."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-conflict-on-merging-main-runs-mains-definition-and-keeps-ours}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-task-carries-what-it-names"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a merge task merges main into the work branch whole (`seon.agent/merge-main!`) and the writer merges the whole branch, scratch helpers included. Owner, 2026-10-22: nothing moves between branches by default. Correct (DESIGN-tasks §3, prototyped: triple conflict, triples copy, nope missing, no scratch carried): :seon.task/carry ([:var sym] [:schema k] [:datum e a]), :seon.task/source, :seon.task/target; the merge branch starts at the target's head; each item judged against the merge base as missing, same, copy or conflict; a carried function's branch-only callees not carried are missing (v1: a reach-chosen list missed 4 chain-only helpers); a conflict entry records the side the branch holds (:seon.merge/kept) so `seon.merge/unmerged` stays open until resolved. Deletes: the merge task's whole-branch merge-main."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-merge-task-judges-each-item seon.merge-test/an-uncarried-branch-only-callee-is-missing}}
 {:seon.task/name "main-is-read-once-per-merge"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.agent/merge-main!` builds (seon.merge/program main data-attrs) twice (agent.clj:202 inside three-way, again as theirs at :204), and seon.writer's branch-merge (writer.clj:22-29) writes the same base/head/main three-way by hand. Correct: one `seon.merge` function [base head main data-attrs] -> {:result :theirs}, main's program read once; merge-main! and branch-merge call it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/after-merge-main-the-agent-judges-against-the-new-base}}
 {:seon.task/name "data-attrs-read-from-the-attribute-index"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.merge/data-attr-set`'s :all case filters `(d/schema db)` for the data attributes. d/schema rebuilds the whole schema map on every call: about 0.8 MB at 365 attributes, more as the schema grows (datahike.api.impl/schema, a reduce-kv over the db's schema). It runs once per db per read: `seon.merge/touched-keys` reads it on base and on each db, and `seon.merge/program-of`, `seon.merge/merge-tx` and `seon.merge/program` read it again, so a merge costs about 0.8 MB x (1 + dbs) before it reads one changed datom. Agents merge main at every green, so every run pays it. The same mistake in seon.render/one, seon.task/carry-tx and seon.config/from-db was fixed by reading the db's attribute index (datahike.db.utils/ref?, /multival?; merge eedad28ccb: carry-tx at 100 tasks went from 774 MB to 3.09 MB). Correct: the data attributes come from the sets the db already holds, datahike.db.interface/-attrs-by of each value type or rschema's per-property sets (the way touched-keys already reads `:db.cardinality/many`), filtered by namespace (program/attr-namespaces, platform-record?), with no map rebuilt per call. Write the cost proof first and watch it go red on today's code: a merge's bytes on a db whose schema grows with n attributes and one changed datom are flat in the attributes once the read is the index's. Deletes nothing; src about +-0."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-cost-test/a-merge-reads-the-data-attributes-from-the-index}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store" :seon.task/plan "Whether a Claude worktree branch can merge through the store (the design in claude_scratch/archive/merge-audit-2026-10-27.md; the review claude_scratch/archive/merge-design-review-2026-10-27.md says: on hold until experiment 3's diff is empty). Measured (claude_scratch/merge-probe-2026-10-27.md; probe root on 4840ddb's first parent, jolt/data-attrs-from-the-index imported by `seon.source/refresh-tx`): experiment 1 holds on correctness: :ours of `seon.merge/delta-three-way` is exactly the definitions git's diff touches (10 vars, 16 forms), 0 conflicts, 67 datoms written for the branch, `seon.source/files` byte-equal to git show tip for both files; but it cost 12,607 calls and 277 MB, not about 39 and under 600 KB (main's side changed 79 keys since the base, and the values were d/db-with copies, not store branches: the number on real d/branch! branches is not measured). Experiment 2 fails: the merged program's 917 tests all plan to run, 0 reused from test-results/ that bin/prove --merge proved green; the first differing digest is datahike.query-test/a-nested-call-in-a-predicate-is-refused (import 11baa001 against 5 stored greens). Cause, partial: every digest takes :env, which digests `seon.fx/config-input`, the whole config in force less :seon.config/place keys; the probe root's config was not bin/test's (a --set of agent max-running, its own --config layer); restoring that key changed the digest and still left 917. Next probe: compare bin/test --plan's per-test digests on 4840ddb's tree with the import's, one by one. Experiment 3, not run: on a worktree root (its own store, never runs/store, :seon.config.source/branch set to a probe-only branch so write-back cannot touch jolt/merges or jolt/seon-merges), attach a task, hand back a past branch that touched src, test, default.edn and a bin script and deleted something, through seon_attach and seon_done; measure whether the judge ran on the imported code (the review: the agent's hand-back runs the proofs on its loaded program, so the import must be the agent's own, as `seon.agent/load-tests!` does, before hand-back), what the verdict tree held against git show tip (default.edn, bin and deps.edn are not in the store), and the write-back commit's tree diff against the branch tip, file modes included (`seon.fx/git-commit` writes every file 100644 and cannot delete). Routing Claude worktrees through the store waits until that diff is empty." :seon.task/kind :work :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/an-outside-branch-writes-back-its-own-tree}}
 {:seon.task/name "touched-keys-skips-platform-records"
  :seon.task/plan "An agent's merge-readiness read (```seon.agent/merge-readiness```, ```seon.merge/delta-three-way```, every turn of a merge task) and its branch changes (```seon.merge/delta-changes```) took one :eavt datoms-diff of base and a db and filtered the differing datoms by attribute after, so it walked every datom that differs, the platform's too: on a worktree root whose main embedded 4,363 forms after the agent forked, 22,420 datoms (21,815 of them the five seon.vector attributes), 313M ticks and 640 MB of the 368M-tick, 888 MB request build, for no key. ```seon.merge/touched-keys``` now walks one :aevt range per attribute a key's content reads and per side, the program's (```seon.program/attr-namespaces```) and the declared data names (data-attrs a set), so the platform's records are not walked: 5.4M ticks and 19 MB on the same databases, the same 17 keys (old and new run side by side), the whole request build 368M to about 60M. data-attrs :all (every attribute the store declares) keeps the one :eavt diff, whose cost does not follow the attributes' count (seon.merge-cost-test/a-merge-reads-the-data-attributes-from-the-index). The fixed part is now the descents: about 59 attributes x two sides at 60-70 KB (3.5 MB when nothing differs, seon.merge-cost-test/a-main-that-only-embedded-costs-nothing is constant in the forms main embedded: 0.67 MB at 10 forms and 63 MB at 1,000 before), and past 1,000 forms each descent gains a tree level (14.1 MB at 10,000). One walk over all the ranges (a multi-prefix datoms-diff in our Datahike, task seon.reconcile/one-observe-descends-once) would descend once. Also fixed with it: ```seon.merge/changed-vars``` destructured each key one level too deep, so it threw on any change set that held a key (seon.problems/caused-split and the test plan call it): seon.merge-test/divergence-names-each-change-a-merge-carries and a-branch-that-merged-main-carries-only-its-own-changes were red on main."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-cost-test/a-main-that-only-embedded-costs-nothing seon.merge-cost-test/a-three-way-costs-the-changes-not-the-program seon.merge-test/divergence-names-each-change-a-merge-carries seon.merge-test/a-branch-that-merged-main-carries-only-its-own-changes}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-merge-system"
  :seon.task/plan "The open design (plan direction 3; this task holds claude_scratch/archive/merge-audit-2026-10-27.md and its review claude_scratch/archive/merge-design-review-2026-10-27.md): one merge system from what Seon already has, not two. Facts (main 4840ddb153): since 2026-10-20 main took 301 first-parent merges, all by git (worktree merges and bin/prove --merge); Seon's own merge (octopus plus write-back) made 14 Seon merge commits, all only on jolt/seon-merges (the review: 0 in main). Duplications the audit named: two mains (git and the store, synced by seon.source/refresh-tx on watch and by write-back plus bin/update); two verdicts (the judge defers process tests and runs them only through my.root/gate!; bin/prove --merge runs every key with no green) and a third runner, seon.prove, the test service (the review: the design never says what becomes of it, and the :tests effect would need a new contract and a lock); two serializers (bin/env's flock and the reconciler's one merge flight); two records (facts and claude_scratch/merge-log.md); a merge task per work task (done: seon.reconcile/a-work-task-merges-itself-through-the-one-gate); three branch-vs-base diffs (seon.merge/delta-changes, seon.program/divergence, seon.program/diff-plan, the last for loading). Owner rulings that bound it: Seon's merge path stays whole and is exercised before anything is cut (do not throw out the baby); a work task merges itself; worktrees retire. Review corrections to apply to the design before anything is built: octopus and merge-keys are not dead under max-running 1 (that caps only :merge-kind starts; whole tasks in one judge pass merge as one octopus), so cutting to one branch is a behaviour change; gate-checkout is set by no local.edn (the gate has never run on the standing root); the branch-key mismatch is worktree roots writing the shared ref (done: bin/env names jolt/seon-merges-<worktree>); the import must be the agent's own before hand-back (seon.agent/load-tests!), not root's after it; the verdict tree must be the branch's own tree because default.edn, system.edn, tasks, bin and deps.edn are not in the store; seon.fx/git-commit writes mode 100644 and cannot delete (a bin script loses its executable bit, deletions are routine), so pin bumps, native code and deletions stay git merges until it can; a Seon merge commit with no branch parent makes the branch look unmerged and drops its measured commit message; a synchronous seon_done running process tests on every merge overturns the 2026-10-23 process-tests ruling and needs the owner. Order: the deletions that stand alone first (done: file tasks merge themselves; the gate and :unrun problem and the merge-log as a render of facts remain); routing worktrees through the store waits on seon.merge/an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store (experiment 3). Correct: the design names which runner (seon.prove or bin/prove) is the one verdict and deletes the other's verdict role, and carries the two experiments' numbers. Proof to write: after the build, no second verdict path is left (a resolve of the gate's vars is nil) and one result store answers both bin/test and the judge (0 keys to run for a tree bin/prove --merge proved)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.merge/an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store"}}]
